WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:07.880
Edward stood frozen in the doorway of the farmhouse. "How did Canty and Hugo find me?"

2
00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:13.265
he wondered in dread. He looked around desperately for an escape route. But the two men

3
00:00:13.266 --> 00:00:20.265
were nearly at the door. Just then the girls' mother held out a pail of

4
00:00:20.266 --> 00:00:22.943
food scraps. "Girls, take this out to the pigs." Edward jumped up and grabbed the pail.

5
00:00:22.944 --> 00:00:26.943
"I can do that, ma'am," he said, running out the back door. Outside, he left the

6
00:00:26.944 --> 00:00:32.618
pail near the barn door and then took off into the woods. He ran as fast

7
00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.520
as he could, never looking back. At that very moment, Tom Canty was also racing. But

8
00:00:36.521 --> 00:00:46.340
unlike Edward he was hurrying through the warm and sumptuous corridors of the palace. Breakfast

9
00:00:46.341 --> 00:00:53.340
had taken place in front of a large group of foreign dignitaries, but Tom had barely

10
00:00:53.341 --> 00:01:01.088
eaten anything. Eating in front of others still made him nervous, so he had devised a

11
00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:08.413
way to eat alone in order not to starve. Entering his private chamber, Tom dismissed the

12
00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:14.809
servants in a decisive manner, which he had learned to do by now. Then, making sure

13
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:22.644
he was alone, he went over to the suit of armor that stood by the

14
00:01:22.645 --> 00:01:28.562
door. As quietly as he could, Tom removed the gauntlet and shook out a few nuts

15
00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:34.060
that he had secretly stored there. From the other gauntlet, he then removed the object that

16
00:01:34.061 --> 00:01:42.218
he had been using to crack open the nuts. "Alone at last!" Tom sighed contentedly as

17
00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:50.695
he crunched on the nuts. But his moment of peace didn't last long. Hertford soon found

18
00:01:50.696 --> 00:01:57.520
him and ushered him away to another boring meeting. Edward ran deeper into the woods.

19
00:01:57.521 --> 00:02:01.708
It was unlikely that Canty and Hugo had spotted him and followed, but he didn't want

20
00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:07.848
to take any chances. Soon the woods grew dark and chilly, and Edward kept tripping over

21
00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:14.180
roots and briars. He knew it wouldn't be long until he needed food and a place

22
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:20.258
to sleep. After what felt like hours, he finally spotted a flicker of light through the

23
00:02:20.259 --> 00:02:29.014
darkness. "This may be my only hope," he said, forcing his tired legs to carry

24
00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:38.922
him toward the light. As he drew nearer, Edward saw that the light came from a

25
00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:44.492
small, shabby cabin. He pressed his face against a grimy window. Inside the main room, there

26
00:02:44.493 --> 00:02:46.833
was a bed off to the side and a stove in the middle. On the other

27
00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:52.397
side of the room, he could see a religious shrine. An old man with long, white

28
00:02:52.398 --> 00:02:58.250
hair knelt there, apparently praying. "A hermit!" Edward thought. "How fortunate that I have found

29
00:02:58.251 --> 00:03:02.740
someone!" He knocked on the man's door. "Enter!" The hermit called. "Unless you are a sinner!"

30
00:03:02.741 --> 00:03:09.725
"Pardon me?" Edward said. The hermit rose from his knees and came closer. Now Edward could

31
00:03:09.726 --> 00:03:17.796
see an odd gleam in the man's eyes. Edward's instincts told him to run, but the

32
00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:24.629
smell of food cooking kept his feet rooted to the ground. "This is a holy place,"

33
00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:38.120
said the hermit. "Only those pure in heart may walk through this door." "I never

34
00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:48.274
lie, so I am pure," Edward declared. "My name is Edward." The old man smiled. "Then

35
00:03:48.275 --> 00:03:54.931
enter! Many have come before you seeking shelter, but they were not worthy. However, you, my

36
00:03:54.932 --> 00:03:59.761
son, are welcome in my home!" Edward stepped closer to the stove to warm himself. He

37
00:03:59.762 --> 00:04:03.670
tried not to stare as the hermit paced around nervously, muttering strange things to himself. "I

38
00:04:03.671 --> 00:04:12.351
freed myself from the outlaws," thought Edward, "only to end up with a madman!" "Will

39
00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:19.765
you join me in a bowl of soup and bread?" the man asked. "Yes, I would

40
00:04:19.766 --> 00:04:28.820
be delighted," Edward answered. As Edward ate the soup, he relaxed a little. "He is an

41
00:04:28.821 --> 00:04:33.828
old man," he told himself. "He can do me no harm." "You are not from these

42
00:04:33.829 --> 00:04:44.640
parts," remarked the hermit. "There is something noble about you." Edward nodded. "That is true.

43
00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:47.573
I have been the victim of a mistaken identity, though no one believes my story. What

44
00:04:47.574 --> 00:04:54.818
about you? Have you lived in these woods a long time?" The hermit laughed. "It has

45
00:04:54.819 --> 00:04:59.809
been too long for me. But I am a patient man. I must allow the Lord

46
00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:11.383
to direct me when it is time." "Time for what?" Edward asked. "Time for my revenge."

47
00:05:11.384 --> 00:05:15.214
The hermit lowered his voice, his eyes glowing in the firelight. "Revenge against whom?" said

48
00:05:15.215 --> 00:05:23.302
Edward, worried now. "The one who forced me here," replied the hermit. "The one who turned

49
00:05:23.303 --> 00:05:28.435
us all out into the world and made us homeless!" He rubbed his bony white hands

50
00:05:28.436 --> 00:05:36.609
together. Edward looked away, silently finishing his soup. "So you say that you have been mistaken

51
00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.010
for someone," the hermit said. "Who might that be?" "A beggar boy whose clothes I am

52
00:05:40.011 --> 00:05:45.436
wearing," Edward said with a sigh. "While I am homeless and penniless, that boy is

53
00:05:45.437 --> 00:05:49.845
dressed in my finery and living in my home." "Where is that home?" asked the hermit.

54
00:05:49.846 --> 00:05:53.580
"At the palace. You see, I am the king." "The king?" The hermit looked startled. "The

55
00:05:53.581 --> 00:05:58.912
king of what?" "Of England, of course," answered Edward. The man grew silent, a dark and

56
00:05:58.913 --> 00:06:01.999
brooding look spreading over his face. Edward felt a chill travel up his spine. Then the

57
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:09.923
hermit spoke again. "That cannot be. The king is called Henry," he whispered. "Yes, he

58
00:06:09.924 --> 00:06:20.489
is&#8212;or was&#8212;my father," Edward said quickly. "But he passed away. And now I am the king."

59
00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:26.909
"So you are the king of England," the hermit repeated, his eyes narrowing. Edward braced himself,

60
00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.643
unsure of what was coming next. Abruptly, the man's demeanor changed. He bowed to Edward. "Forgive

61
00:06:31.644 --> 00:06:36.730
me, Your Majesty. Perhaps you would like a cup of tea." "All right . . ."

62
00:06:36.731 --> 00:06:43.379
Edward said uneasily. "Thank you." The hermit prepared some tea, and after they had drunk

63
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.463
it, the old man spent a long time doctoring the cuts and bruises all over Edward's

64
00:06:47.464 --> 00:06:57.116
arms and legs. He spoke softly and gently as he worked. From time to time, he

65
00:06:57.117 --> 00:07:04.609
even reached out to pat Edward's cheek. "This man is strange, but he's kind," Edward told

66
00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:13.116
himself. Soon he felt very sleepy. The hermit led him to a bed in an adjoining

67
00:07:13.117 --> 00:07:20.442
room, and within minutes, Edward's eyes began to close. The hermit bent over him. "So

68
00:07:20.443 --> 00:07:25.018
Henry is dead." "Yes . . ." Edward murmured sleepily. "He's gone and now I am

69
00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:34.678
. . ." Edward drifted off. The hermit waited several minutes to be sure that Edward

70
00:07:34.679 --> 00:07:39.263
had fallen asleep. Then he got up and searched the cabin until he found what he

71
00:07:39.264 --> 00:07:47.590
was looking for: a rusty old knife and a sharpening stone. The hermit crept back to

72
00:07:47.591 --> 00:07:52.991
his spot near the fire and began sharpening the blade. Hours passed as he worked

73
00:07:52.992 --> 00:07:58.245
on the knife, muttering to himself all the while. At last he returned to Edward's bedside.

74
00:07:58.246 --> 00:08:09.025
His eyes glinted as he gripped the knife and hissed, "Prepare to die, King of England!"

